
 Default Return Type and Prime Check
 
-->What happens if we don't mention any return type while designing a function?
The compiler considers the default return type as int (integer).

Example: set_bit(num) with no type written is treated as int set_bit(num).
(Modern C compilers warn about this, so always write the return type.)

Program: Check whether a number is prime
Design a function to check whether a number is prime or not. If prime, the function returns 1, otherwise 0.

#include <stdio.h>
int prime_check(int n)
{
    int i;
    for (i = 2; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (n % i == 0)          // user defined function
            break;
    }
    if (n == i)
        return 1;
    else
        return 0;
}

int main()
{
    int num, r;
    printf("Enter int num: ");
    scanf("%d", &num);

    r = prime_check(num);            // main function
    if (r)
        printf("prime\n");
    else
        printf("not prime\n");

    return 0;
}


Two ways to use the returned value

Store it in a variable: r = prime_check(num); if (r) ...
Call it directly in the condition: if (prime_check(num)) ...
How it works: the loop stops early (break) if i divides n. If no divisor is found, the loop ends with i == n, so the number is prime.

Call by Value 
 
Program: Swap two numbers (call by value)

What is call by value?
- At the time of calling the function, we supply the value as the actual argument.
Whatever changes we make to the formal argument will not modify the actual argument.


#include <stdio.h>
void swap_num(int, int);
int main()
{
    int i = 10, j = 20;
    printf("Before: i=%d j=%d\n", i, j);
    swap_num(i, j);
    printf("After: i=%d j=%d\n", i, j);
    return 0;
}

void swap_num(int a, int b)
{
    int t;
    t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
}
ans: no output

Answer
Internally it swaps the numbers (a and b), but we can't see it in main().
Before: i=10 j=20
After:  i=10 j=20


Call by Reference

Program: Swap two numbers (call by reference)

What is call by reference?
- At the time of calling the function, we supply the address and catch it using pointers in the function.
When we modify the formal argument, indirectly the actual argument will also be modified.

#include <stdio.h>
void swap_num(int *, int *);   // user function declaration

int main()
{
    int i = 10, j = 20;
    printf("Before: i=%d j=%d\n", i, j);
    swap_num(&i, &j);
    printf("After: i=%d j=%d\n", i, j);
    return 0;
}

void swap_num(int *a, int *b)
{
    int t;
    t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}


swap_num(1000, 2000) passes the addresses, and *a / *b reach the original i and j.
Answer
Before: i=10 j=20
After:  i=20 j=10


 
                          Call by value          vs              call by reference

                          Call by value                        Call by reference/address
What is passed:              Value                                  Address
Formal argument:          Normal variable                           Pointer
Original changed?:            No                                      Yes

